f 17592186045417] [17592186050705 #edb/ref 1031 "Computing Areas Using a Change of Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050705 #edb/ref 1033 1998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050705 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6047b0c9-9ae0-5fc8-9179-8ce5172d5c9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1031 "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1033 1979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050822 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133648 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133648 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0,$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51467bae-f181-53ba-adf2-1218dc16275f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133648 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/3d/3d67d73d3ede011d6c2fe4bc9e160aa3c2af522bff6cddfcc7c351c97418e764.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{4-x^2} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133649 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7025" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133649 #edb/ref 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3,$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133649 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f443df5-1c26-5af4-9541-e83522126197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133649 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/f8/f8a5fd7fc3e98d7f08b5ea48636e5de4a1a45b7a94fbe9e968789afcfe3a582c.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =3x \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are $0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{3}.$ Note that we disregarded the solution $x=-\\sqrt 3$ since $x\\geq 0$ in our region.\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/23/232f885d2230694bf62f81f5987cd2b3a12a49430d90d2cae4712c552587a48c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{3}, \\quad x^3 \\leq y \\leq 3x \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{3x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{3x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133650 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133650 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133650 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b151be22-91a1-5303-b86d-4f9ba748f000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133650 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133651 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133651 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133651 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24dd94c8-2821-5614-9ccf-43c3656267f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133651 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $y \\leq 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny = -\\sqrt{4-x^2}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/31/31e7bb281a36a3676730de0883d7abd5445093713de1bbd41899cce50a7e0e48.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$-2 \\le x \\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/2a/2a84d17b83fb1e2036c6edc0a71c5c10a58bf893c33bbf38662ebb417aab3164.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD = \\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad -\\sqrt{4-x^2} \\leq y \\leq 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-2}^2 \\int_{-\\sqrt{4-x^2}}^{0} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}x[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x(0-(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x(-(4-{x}^{2}))\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({x}^{2}-4)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}-4x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}-4x)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-2{x}^{2}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((4-8)-(4-8)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing a Double Integral as a Repe