 17592186045417] [17592186134308 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/da/da2895c09bc33f628626257b6d102e74875825df14ba744904e02ffa71552a02.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1+{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134517 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=2{x}^{2}.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f61d37a-849f-5ed6-81a5-d25f65061240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134517 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/4f/4fec059132b7bd02cb64f864b6982c0c64a66c22646334ca199fad12097ba435.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134753 #edb/ref 1061 25905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a5c014d-2a5f-5ef2-b451-9d6636c3241e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134754 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134754 #edb/ref 1061 25906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3edf61a9-ab06-533a-ad5f-906f95245bb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134755 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134755 #edb/ref 1061 25907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134755 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "621aac0e-49f3-510c-b684-d0f438398280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134755 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134756 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134756 #edb/ref 1061 25908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134756 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59bc86e8-4dae-564b-ab2b-c8ac5309d3be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134756 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134757 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134757 #edb/ref 1061 25909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134757 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9095d8fd-3ce2-5af6-bda4-4a49a820addf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134757 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134758 #edb/ref 1061 25911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134758 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb2a0a7b-340c-53a3-a510-9a559cc6d898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134758 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135082 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we represent the double integral of a function over a non-rectangular region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n![](images/3a/3ac99dd8336c3a877fbf6ba63a3074a490b85c8a350adad00da423412dd60ee3.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg)  is the finite region enclosed by the lines ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg), ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg), ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg) and ![](images/b5/b5489c125683eb29c2cababe2e1b7094d6a16b89d09970c42af649df70d045e4.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/af0a1a74df8aec0d5189dc2402fc1840c25d4cd94d8861fa06110cae7b449843.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a *type I* region. \nTo determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/39/39629b33e25aed05bf9a5ca36180f984e7ff8f863c6966d47f60483d672a8af4.png)\n\nSo, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be defined as \n\n![](images/78/78cf2ea7f960497f7cc9beea602bf6ca0e82b74491f9f56cce76130d965d4a64.svg)\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](images/3c/3c423b0d423203621ab89258a6fff4cea190279b4dcc7170c8a7070bf1f85e24.svg)\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that, when we have a type I region, \n\n- the limits for ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) are functions of ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg)\n\n- the limits for ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) are constants, and\n\n- we integrate over the variable ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) before integrating over the variable ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135082 #edb/ref 1065 4261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135082 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7c641021-bc5e-5b05-8a23-b198735ee5a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135082 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135083 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{x}^{2x} (x-2y) \\ \\mathrm{d}  y \\ \\mathrm{d}  x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type I region. \n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n\n- When integrating with respect to $y,$ we treat $x$ as though it were a constant.\n\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $x.$ Once again, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $y$'s in our integral.\n\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{2x}(x-2y)\\text{ }dy]\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[xy-{y}^{2}]_{x}^{2x}\\text{ }dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x\\cdot (2x)-{(2x)}^{2}]-[x\\cdot (x)-(x)^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}]-[{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx & =-2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135083 #edb/ref 1065 4262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135083 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3698e7e3-4bc2-5db0-96b5-172131284b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135083 #edb/ref 1067 "Evaluating Repeated Integrals With Variable Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267638 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267638 #edb/ref 1082