326494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "999d4563-89d1-59c3-ad40-f8521bd60953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326875 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326875 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326875 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326875 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/ce364dc7c6c4fc5e229fdeb0809b3779bf1052c60d956deb742731f34e54562d.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=2x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=4,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326875 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8348ba1-d0ae-5fbd-ab2d-88304b94eb8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326875 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/54/54f366679684823341bab818459ce5fc884ebbdd2376e800b781d1b832c4ba64.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2x\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326882 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/93/933e68e42fe61af3e30fae012b0b56dce6793b0fadc343e04a0766b85cb6ae7e.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=9-{x}^{2}$ and $y=1+{x}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74e8d822-2417-5c6e-b5f9-e86873e83a1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326882 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/c5/c56c5be4ac88755f1859f295f700be8710d75b16380ac120e6b56a3aa767acbe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,1+{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 9-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{9-{x}^{2}}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326883 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326883 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326883 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326883 #edb/ref 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and $x=4,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326883 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c444980-62b8-5433-81a1-2731b5e581cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326883 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/fc/fcb347767983c38eb12f521ca556dcc5a96705ee4e887fb8d43c42984dfe1a26.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{x})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/91/91bf7087e5aa0053d24cb45158d48b0bb36fb84b28ebabaeba3c26dc62168dfe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/x}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345400 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{2}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{2{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345400 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345400 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05758313-2849-5382-9981-f8c14bba7351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345946 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{2}^{2{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345946 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345946 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "44c43d75-bbbd-5904-9b9a-2fa603f1e3c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346343 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346343 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346343 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70ef54df-e3cb-539c-b762-c8ec8074da07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346354 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346354 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346354 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7181df6d-b8d5-5be8-bc00-55ae72cc7fc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346968 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\